  "answer_text": "Here is a comprehensive overview of the effects of climate change on ocean life:\n\n---\n\n## \ud83c\udf0a Effects of Climate Change on Ocean Life\n\n### 1. \ud83c\udf21\ufe0f Rising Ocean Temperatures & Marine Heatwaves\nLife in most of the global ocean, from pole to pole and from sea surface to the abyssal depths, is already experiencing higher temperatures due to human-driven climate change. Warmer sea temperatures disturb the sensitive balance within ocean habitats and affect where and how sea creatures live and act.\n\nMarine heatwaves can be devastating \u2014 for example, due to the 2019\u20132021 Pacific Northwest marine heatwave, Bering Sea snow crab populations declined 84% between 2018 and 2022, a loss of 9.8 billion crabs. Scientists predict that the frequency, duration, scale, and intensity of marine heatwaves will increase, as sea surface temperatures will continue to rise with global warming.\n\n---\n\n### 2. \ud83e\uddea Ocean Acidification\nIncreased carbon dioxide in the air makes oceans more acidic. This occurs when seawater absorbs carbon dioxide and mixes it with water to form carbonic acid, decreasing ocean pH.\n\nThe increased acid in the water can badly affect sea creatures that need calcium carbonate to make their shells and bones. These creatures also act as carbon stores, which helps mitigate climate change. But as their shells dissolve, this natural way of removing carbon from the atmosphere is threatened.\n\n---\n\n### 3. \ud83d\udc20 Coral Reef Destruction\nDubbed the \"rainforests of the sea,\" coral reefs are home to more than 25% of marine life in the world, including more than 4,000 fish species, as well as turtles, plants, invertebrates, and marine mammals.\n\nAs climate change disrupts the delicate balance of ecosystems within coral reefs, it leads to coral death. Without coral, many of the species that inhabit the reefs lack food and shelter, facing fatal outcomes. A warming of 1.5\u00b0C threatens to destroy 70 to 90 percent of coral reefs, and a 2\u00b0C increase means a nearly 100 percent loss \u2014 a point of no return.\n\n---\n\n### 4. \ud83d\udca7 Ocean Deoxygenation\nOverall ocean oxygen concentrations are estimated to have declined 2% over 50 years from the 1960s. Low oxygen represents a stress for almost all marine animals. Very low oxygen levels create regions with much-reduced fauna, and it is predicted that these low oxygen zones will expand in the future due to climate change, posing a serious threat to marine life.\n\n---\n\n### 5. \ud83d\udd17 Disruption of Food Chains\nOne of the most critical changes brought about by global warming is the ongoing reduction of phytoplankton. These tiny plants are an integral food source for ocean life and are responsible for around half of the world's photosynthetic activity. They are the foundations of the oceanic food chain, so a reduction in their numbers creates a knock-on effect that ripples up the entire food chain, particularly affecting predators at the top.\n\n---\n\n### 6. \ud83d\udccd Species Migration & Biodiversity Loss\nWidespread changes have been observed, including damage to coral reefs and mangroves that support ocean life, and migration of species to higher latitudes and altitudes where the water could be cooler.\n\nLatest estimates from UNESCO warn that more than half of the world's marine species may stand on the brink of extinction by 2100.\n\n---\n\n### 7. \u26a0\ufe0f The \"Deadly Trio\"\nClimate change's \"deadly trio\" \u2014 increased global warming, ocean acidification, and increased deoxygenation \u2014 when occurring simultaneously, have synergetic effects that damage marine life and ecosystem structure and functions. This leads to biodiversity loss and degraded ecosystem functions \u2014 often through non-linear, cascading effects. In parallel, ecosystem services suffer, including reduced catches of commercially important fisheries, and blue carbon ecosystems losing their capacity to store carbon.\n\n---\n\n### The Big Picture\nA study published in 2025 projected that rising ocean temperatures, together with other climate-driven stressors, will more than double cumulative impacts on marine ecosystems by mid-century. The health of our oceans is deeply intertwined with the health of our planet, making addressing climate change a critical priority for the preservation of marine life.",
